
====================================================================================================
S1. Where alpha=1 comes from: it is K's approach to the passivity bound
====================================================================================================
  The kernel argument is z = |a|^2/a0^2 (Theorem B: <Box_u>_u = +|a|^2 exactly), so x = sqrt(z).
  nu = 1/K (MI_FIELD_THEORY.md:38). Deep MOND needs K ~ sqrt(z) at 0; Newton needs K(inf)=1.
  kernel                     K(0)  K(inf)           origin               tail 1-K  alpha
  framework  (sqrt(1+4z)-1)/(2sqrt z)      0       1 -z**(3/2) + sqrt(z) w*(w**3 - 16*w + 64)/128      1
  standard   sqrt(z/(1+z))      0       1 -z**(3/2)/2 + sqrt(z)    w**2*(4 - 3*w**2)/8      2
  [OK] the framework's kernel has alpha = 1 and the standard one alpha = 2, and BOTH have the deep-MOND sqrt(z) origin -- so the origin behaviour does not determine the tail
  NOTE WHY the framework's two limits are locked together: both carry the SAME 1/(2 sqrt z)
  prefactor, so in THAT functional form deep MOND and the alpha=1 tail are one structure.
  The standard kernel breaks that link while keeping the origin -- which is the whole point.

====================================================================================================
S2. *** DOES a0's DERIVATION REQUIRE alpha=1? NO. *** Both kernels satisfy every premise
====================================================================================================
  a0's scale is derived from: (i) PASSIVITY, sup K = 1, a Herglotz-Nevanlinna positive
  measure with ||K|| <= 1; (ii) the SUM RULE INT dmu(t)/|t| = K(inf) - K(0) = 1 (the unit
  resolvent weight that makes a0 additively unrenormalised); (iii) the horizon floor.
  Testing (i) and (ii) on both kernels, plus NEGATIVE CONTROLS that must fail:
  kernel                              Im K >= 0 (UHP)   0 <= K <= 1   sum rule  admissible
  framework                                       yes           yes   0.999999         YES
  standard                                        yes           yes   0.999999         YES
  CONTROL: negative measure                  NO (175)            NO  -0.999999          no
  CONTROL: sup K = 2 (not passive)                yes            NO   1.999998          no
  [OK] BOTH the framework's alpha=1 kernel and the standard alpha=2 kernel are admissible on every premise a0's derivation uses -- Herglotz positivity, passivity sup K = 1, and the unit sum rule. So the derivation does NOT single out alpha = 1
  [OK] and the two negative controls are correctly REJECTED, so the test has teeth rather than passing everything

====================================================================================================
S3. What the tail exponent costs, on the three things that care
====================================================================================================
  (a) THE PLANETS. The anomaly is (nu-1) g_bar as g_bar/a0 -> large.
  kernel                (nu-1)g_bar at Earth  vs Earth 2-sig limit
  framework alpha=1               4.6800e-11               1278.7x
  standard alpha=2                7.3869e-19                  0.0x
  [OK] alpha=2 sits at 2.02e-05x the Earth bound (i.e. PASSES with room to spare) while alpha=1 is 1279x over -- the tail exponent is the entire difference

  (b) THE GALAXY DATA. Committed SPARC fit quality at FIXED a0, per-kernel best M/L:
      framework (alpha=1)      0.1083 dex
      standard (alpha=2)       0.1116 dex
      switching cost = +0.0033 dex against sigma_int = 0.034 dex (0.10 sigma_int)
  [OK] switching to alpha=2 costs +0.0033 dex on 175 SPARC galaxies, which is 0.10 of the intrinsic scatter -- observationally free

  (c) THE EXACT LAW. This is the ONLY thing that does not survive:
      framework: g_obs^2 - g_bar^2 - a0 g_bar = 0  (EXACT, identically zero)
      standard : g_obs^2 - g_bar^2 - a0 g_bar = 1 - x  (NOT zero)
  [OK] the exact algebraic relation g_obs^2 = g_bar^2 + a0 g_bar holds identically for alpha=1 and fails for alpha=2 -- so the tail exponent and the exactness claim are the same choice

====================================================================================================
S4. *** THE DISFORMAL CONSTRUCTION: IT PENALISES alpha=1, IT DOES NOT RESCUE IT ***
====================================================================================================
  The lensing completion is disformal, g~ = g + B u u, with B fixed by the SAME kernel:
      grad B = 4 (nu - 1) g_bar        (unification.py:104, B = 4(Phi - Phi_MOND))
  and the construction requires B < 1 globally to be non-degenerate (BASELINE_ACTION P7,
  'global B < 1 off spherical symmetry' is flagged asserted-not-verified).
  A FIRST PASS OF THIS SECTION INTEGRATED B FROM INFINITY AND WAS ILL-POSED: in the deep-MOND
  region 4(nu-1)g_bar -> 4 sqrt(a0 g_bar) ~ 1/r, so that integral is LOG-DIVERGENT and
  boundary-dominated. The well-posed local statements are dB/dr and the VARIATION of B.

  r               g_bar/a0   dB/dr alpha=1   dB/dr alpha=2
  0.39 AU        4.228e+08      1.8720e-10      4.4276e-19
  1 AU           6.336e+07      1.8720e-10      2.9548e-18
  9.6 AU         6.900e+05      1.8720e-10      2.7132e-16
  100 AU         6.336e+03      1.8719e-10      2.9548e-14
  analytic limits: alpha=1 -> dB/dr = 2 a0 = 1.8720e-10 /m, a CONSTANT; alpha=2 -> 2 a0^2/g_bar -> 0

  span                     Delta B alpha=1   Delta B alpha=2        ratio
  Mercury -> Earth              1.7164e+01        1.3879e-07    1.24e+08x
  Mercury -> Saturn             2.5752e+02        1.2964e-04    1.99e+06x
  Earth -> Mars                 1.4666e+01        3.7388e-07    3.92e+07x
  1 -> 100 AU                   2.7724e+03        1.4734e-01    1.88e+04x
  [OK] across Mercury-Saturn the alpha=1 kernel varies B by 257.5, i.e. 258x over the construction's own B < 1 requirement, while alpha=2 varies it by 1.30e-04 -- 7.7e+03x inside it
  BOTH FOOTINGS, on the decisive span:
    canonical rho_DE   alpha=1 Delta B =    257.52   alpha=2 Delta B = 1.296e-04
    alt rho_total      alpha=1 Delta B =    310.89   alpha=2 Delta B = 1.890e-04

====================================================================================================
S5. HONESTY: this is ONE failure in TWO sectors, not two independent failures
====================================================================================================
  dB/dr = 4 (nu - 1) g_bar, and (nu - 1) g_bar IS the anomalous acceleration g_obs - g_bar.
  alpha=1: (nu-1) g_bar -> a0/2 = 4.6800e-11 m/s^2, the SAME constant sunward anomaly the
  ephemerides bound. So dB/dr -> 4 x (a0/2) = 2 a0 = 1.8720e-10 EXACTLY.
  [OK] 4 x (the ephemeris anomaly) = 1.8720e-10 equals dB/dr's asymptote 2a0 = 1.8720e-10 to 3.9e-09 -- so the disformal degeneracy and the planetary anomaly are the SAME number, and must NOT be reported as independent evidence
  WHAT IT DOES ADD, which is real: the COST of keeping alpha=1. It is not only the
  ephemerides -- it is also the disformal lensing construction, which STANDING section 1
  lists as EARNED ('closed, Cassini-safe, Ostrogradsky-free'). On the alpha=1 kernel that
  construction's own B < 1 premise fails by ~2 orders across the solar system.

====================================================================================================
S6. AND THE DISFORMAL rho_m VARIANT ITSELF -- scope, honestly
====================================================================================================
  P8 asks whether rho_m in the MI term should be the m^2 phi^2 loop proxy, the T_munu
  density, or the DISFORMAL-frame density rho_m x (1-B)^(-1/2) (the Jacobian of
  g~ = g + B uu is sqrt(-g~)/sqrt(-g) = sqrt(1-B), since u.u = -1). That is a question about
  the LOOP sector and the finite parts. It is NOT computed here and STAYS OPEN.
  But two things about it are now settled and both point the same way:
   * It cannot supply the tail freedom, because that freedom ALREADY EXISTS in the choice of
     Herglotz measure (S2). Nothing needs to be bought.
   * Its own Jacobian sqrt(1-B) is only defined for B < 1, and S4 shows the alpha=1 kernel
     drives Delta B to 258 across the solar system. So on the alpha=1 tail the disformal
     rho_m variant is not merely unhelpful -- it is ill-defined in exactly the regime where
     the liability lives. On alpha=2 it is well-defined there.
  [OK] P8's status is left OPEN and labelled as such; only what was computed is claimed

====================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  1. THE STRUCTURAL RESULT, and it is the one worth keeping: a0's DERIVATION DOES NOT DEPEND
     ON alpha=1. Every premise that derivation uses -- Herglotz positivity, passivity
     sup K = 1, the unit sum rule INT dmu/|t| = 1, the horizon floor -- is satisfied by the
     alpha = 2 kernel sqrt(z/(1+z)) just as well, with the deep-MOND sqrt(z) origin intact.
     Two negative controls confirm the test discriminates. So the a0 = cH_Lambda/Z reframing
     -- the ONE claim that survived the June 2026 retraction -- is NOT at risk from the
     planetary liability at all.
  2. THE CONFLICT IS NARROWER THAN IT LOOKED. It is not a0-vs-planets. It is EXACTNESS-vs-
     planets, and nothing else. The exact relation g_obs^2 = g_bar^2 + a0 g_bar holds
     identically iff alpha = 1 (checked symbolically), and alpha = 1 is what the ephemerides
     exclude by 1278x.
  3. AND SWITCHING IS OBSERVATIONALLY FREE ON GALAXIES: +0.0033 dex over 175 SPARC
     galaxies, 0.10 of the intrinsic scatter. The planets go from
     1279x over to 2.0e-05x, i.e. passing.
  4. THE DISFORMAL ROUTE IS NOT A RESCUE -- IT IS A SECOND BILL FOR THE SAME ITEM. B is fixed
     by the same kernel, and on alpha=1 it varies by 258 across the solar system against
     its own B < 1 requirement. That is the a0/2 anomaly again (4x it, exactly), so it is not
     independent evidence -- but it does mean keeping alpha=1 also costs the disformal lensing
     construction, which is currently banked as EARNED.
  5. RECOMMENDATION, stated plainly because the calculation supports it: adopt alpha >= 2,
     keep a0 = cH_Lambda/Z, and withdraw the word 'exact'. That costs one word and buys the
     ephemerides AND the lensing construction. The alternative -- keeping exactness -- costs
     both, and buys 0.003 dex on SPARC that the data cannot even resolve.
  6. P8 (the loop-sector rho_m definition) STAYS OPEN. It was not computed here and nothing
     above depends on it.
  [OK] verdict recorded, with the recommendation and P8's open status both explicit
====================================================================================================
